<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chapter-accuracy-standard-form">
  <title>Accuracy, Precision, Truncation and Rounding</title>
  <objectives xml:id="li-number-accuracy"><title>Learning Intentions</title><introduction><p>After completing this chapter, you should be able to:</p></introduction><ol>
      <li><p>Explain the difference between exact and approximate quantities.</p></li>
      <li><p>Round to decimal places or significant figures.</p></li>
      <li><p>Distinguish rounding from truncation and interpret measurement precision.</p></li>
    </ol></objectives>
  <introduction><p>Accuracy, precision, truncation and rounding must be distinguished whenever we report an approximation.</p></introduction>
  <section xml:id="accuracy-intro">
    <title>Introduction</title>
    <introduction><p>Measurements and calculations should be reported to a degree of accuracy appropriate to the context. Decimal places, significant figures and rounding describe different kinds of precision.</p></introduction>
    <p>Truncation discards digits beyond a specified place. Rounding selects the value to a specified precision using an agreed rule.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="accuracy-standard-form-practice">
    <title>Worked Example and Practice</title>
    <example xml:id="example-accuracy-standard-form">
      <title>Worked example</title>
      <statement><p>Round 12.786 to two decimal places and truncate it to two decimal places.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>Rounding gives 12.79; truncation gives 12.78.</p></solution>
    </example>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-accuracy-standard-form">
      <statement><p>Round 0.004786 to two significant figures.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>0.0048.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>
</chapter>
